        "explanation": "The two plots show the same DESeq2 data. Both put log2 fold change on the x-axis (about -8.5 to 2) and -log10 adjusted p-value on the y-axis (0 to about 42).\n\n**Same significant genes:** Both plots mark the same five genes as significant, at the same positions:\n- LFC \u2248 -8.1, -log10 padj \u2248 41.5\n- LFC \u2248 -3.85, \u2248 22.8\n- LFC \u2248 -1.7, \u2248 14.5\n- LFC \u2248 -1.95, \u2248 13.5\n- LFC \u2248 1.5, \u2248 3.7\n\n**Same non-significant genes:** In both plots the grey points form a dense cluster near zero. They spread from about -2.4 to 2, and they all fall at or below -log10 padj \u2248 1.3, which corresponds to padj = 0.05. Outlying grey points also appear in the same places, for example near LFC -2.4, -2.0, -1.4 and 1.7\u20132.0.\n\n**Differences, all in presentation:**\n- **Colours:** The reference uses two colours, red for significant and grey for not significant. The candidate splits significant genes by direction: blue for the four down-regulated genes, red for the one up-regulated gene, grey for the rest.\n- **Threshold lines:** The candidate adds dashed lines at LFC = \u00b11 and padj = 0.05.\n- **Labels:** Titles, axis labels and themes differ (theme_minimal vs theme_bw).\n\n**Threshold rule:** The candidate counts a gene as significant only if padj < 0.05 and |LFC| > 1, while the reference uses padj < 0.05 alone. Here all five padj-significant genes also have |LFC| > 1, so both plots classify every gene the same way.\n\nThe guideline requires that colour separate genes with padj < 0.05 from the rest, and the candidate meets it. The scientific message is the same: a few strongly down-regulated genes and one up-regulated gene. The extra |LFC| condition is the only small departure from the guideline; it doesn't change the result for this data."
